ec18ef591f22b438fd6713ed7b5e67aaab645a9379312c9b5149b0c290ff3.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the angle $\\overset{^}{COD}$ (or $\\overset{^}{DOC}$) has a measure of ${30}^{∘}.$" 13194139537005 17592186576411 false] [17592186134120 1074 "Let's highlight the angle with a measure ${30}^{∘}$ on our diagram.\n\n![](images/54/547ec18ef591f22b438fd6713ed7b5e67aaab645a9379312c9b5149b0c290ff3.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the angle $\\widehat{COD}$ (or $\\widehat{DOC}$) has a measure of ${30}^{∘}.$" 13194139537005 17592186576411 true] [17592186134169 1071 "![](images/3c/3c35ebfcad87dca92f765ef586729356a45266292c10e17628a8b2efd0541c06.png)\n\nWhich numbered angle refers to the same angle as $FGD^(^)$?" 13194139537005 17592186576411 false] [17592186134169 1071 "![](images/3c/3c35ebfcad87dca92f765ef586729356a45266292c10e17628a8b2efd0541c06.png)\n\nWhich numbered angle refers to the same angle as $\\widehat{FGD}$?" 13194139537005 17592186576411 true] [17592186134172 1071 "![](images/8e/8edd3796a19599d80703874d8ef4631b4c0c5100f74f6bd4a4d2c138883a3123.png)\n\nWhat is the measure of the angle $CGE^(^)$?" 13194139537005 17592186576411 false] [17592186134172 1071 "![](images/8e/8edd3796a19599d80703874d8ef4631b4c0c5100f74f6bd4a4d2c138883a3123.png)\n\nWhat is the measure of the angle $\\widehat{CGE}$?" 13194139537005 17592186576411 true] [17592186157172 1074 "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with the population mean $\\mu.$ \nThen, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \n= \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i.\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the mean value of $X_1,X_2,\\ldots, X_n,$ while $\\bar{x}$ is the mean of a specific sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$\n\n- the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an estimator of the population mean $\\mu,$ and\n\n- the value $\\hat{\\mu} = \\bar{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean $\\mu.$\n\nTherefore, our estimate for the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\mu } & =\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(6+13+7+5+14+11+7) \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{7} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139537005 17592186576411 true] [17592186157172 1074 "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with the population mean $\\mu.$ \nThen, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \n= \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i.\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the mean value of $X_1,X_2,\\ldots, X_n,$ while $\\bar{x}$ is the mean of a specific sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$\n\n- the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an estimator of the population mean $\\mu,$ and\n\n- the value $\\hat{\\mu} = \\bar{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean $\\mu.$\n\nTherefore, our estimate for the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\mu } & =\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(6+13+7+5+14+11+7) \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{7} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139537005 17592186576411 false] [17592186159789 1080 "E[\\widehat{p}] = 0.45, Var[\\widehat{p}] = 0.0124" 13194139537005 17592186576411 true] [17592186159789 1080 "E[p^(^)] = 0.45, Var[p^(^)] = 0.0124" 13194139537005 17592186576411 false] [17592186159790 1080 "E[\\widehat{p}] = 0.45, Var[\\widehat{p}] = 0.0231" 13194139537005 17592186576411 true] [17592186159790 1080 "E[p^(^)] = 0.45, Var[p^(^)] = 0.0231" 13194139537005 17592186576411 false] [17592186159791 1080 "E[\\widehat{p}] = 0.45, Var[\\widehat{p}] = 0.0175" 13194139537005 17592186576411 true] [17592186159791 1080 "E[p^(^)] = 0.45, Var[p^(^)] = 0.0175" 13194139537005 17592186576411 false] [17592186159792 1080 "E[\\widehat{p}] = 0.55, Var[\\widehat{p}] = 0.0231" 13194139537005 17592186576411 true] [17592186159792 1080 "E[p^(^)] = 0.55, Var[p^(^)] = 0.0231" 13194139537005 17592186576411 false] [17592186159793 1080 "E[\\widehat{p}] = 0.55, Var[\\widehat{p}] = 0.0124" 13194139537005 17592186576411 true] [17592186159793 1080 "E[p^(^)] = 0.55, Var[p^(^)] = 0.0124" 13194139537005 17592186576411 false] [17592186159837 1080 "E[\\widehat{p}] = 0.15, Var[\\widehat{p}] = 0.0036" 13194139537005 17592186576411 true] [17592186159837 1080 "E[p^(^)] = 0.15, Var[p^(^)] = 0.0036" 13194139537005 17592186576411 false] [17592186159838 1080 "E[\\widehat{p}] = 0.15, Var[\\widehat{p}] = 0.0025" 13194139537005 17592186576411 true] [17592186159838 1080 "E[p^(^)] = 0.15, Var[p^(^)] = 0.0025" 13194139537005 17592186576411 false] [17592186159839 1080 "E[\\widehat{p}] = 0.15, Var[\\widehat{p}] = 0.0021" 13194139537005 17592186576411 true] [17592186159839 1080 "E[p^(^)] = 0.15, Var[p^(^)] = 0.0021" 13194139537005 17592186576411 false] [17592186159840 1080 "E[\\widehat{p}] = 0.85, Var[\\widehat{p}] = 0.0021" 13194139537005 17592186576411 true] [17592186159840 1080 "E[p^(^)] = 0.85, Var[p^(^)] = 0.0021" 13194139537005 17592186576411 false] [17592186159841 1080 "E[\\widehat{p}] = 0.85, Var[\\widehat{p}] = 0.0036" 13194139537005 17592186576411 true] [17592186159841 1080 "E[p^(^)] = 0.85, Var[p^(^)] = 0.0036" 13194139537005 17592186576411 false] [17592186161703 1074 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables $X_i$ as follows:\n![](images/41/412ed703d6c44b1e664317fd734871cfe71d14c944cfa9f9bca84752f635836e.svg)\n\nNow, provided that $N \\gg n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\approx B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}  = \\overline{X} = \\dfrac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\widehat{\\,p} ] = p, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p} ] = \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p = 42 \\% = 0.42,$ and $n = 40.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =42%=0.42, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.42(1-0.42)}{40} \\\\  & ≈0.0061\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." 13194139537005 17592186576411 false] [17592186161703 1074 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables $X_i$ as follows:\n![](images/41/412ed703d6c44b1e664317fd734871cfe71d14c944cfa9f9bca84752f635836e.svg)\n\nNow, provided that $N \\gg n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\approx B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}  = \\overline{X} = \\dfrac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\widehat{\\,p} ] = p, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p} ] = \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p = 42 \\% = 0.42,$ and $n = 40.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}] & =42\\%=0.42, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.42(1-0.42)}{40} \\\\  & ≈0.0061\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." 13194139537005 17592186576411 true] [17592186161737 1074 "Suppose we have a set of observed frequencies $O_i$ and corresponding expected frequencies $E_i$, as defined by a theoretical model under the null hypothesis. Then, the random variable\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i} \\frac{(O_i - E_i)^2}{E_i}\n$$\n\napproximately follows a chi-square distribution with $\\nu$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $O_i$ is the observed frequency for category $i$, and\n- $E_i$ is the expected frequency for category $i$, as predicted under the null hypothesis.\n\nThis approximation holds when all expected frequencies $E_i$ are sufficiently large (typically\n\n$$\nE_i \\geq 5\n$$\n\nfor each category), and the observations are independent.\n\nLet $Y$ be the number of customer complaints received per week. Under the null hypothesis, we have that $Y \\sim \\mathrm{Po}(\\lambda)$.\n\nThe number of observations is\n\n$$\nN = 20+12+8 =40\n$$\n\nWe can calculate an estimator for $\\lambda$, which we denote as $\\hat \\lambda$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hat{λ} &= \\frac{total number of events}{total number of observations} \\\\\n&= (∑(y_{i} \\cdot O_{i}))/(N)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $O_i$ is the observed frequency for the $i$th observation, $N$ is the number of observations. So, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hat{λ} &= \\frac{0 \\cdot 20 + 1 \\cdot 12 + 2 \\cdot 8}{40} \\\\\n&= 0.7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the proposed distribution is $Y\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(0.7)$.\n\nUnder the hypothesis that $Y \\sim \\mathrm{Po}(0.7)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Y = y) &= \\frac{λ^{y}e^{- λ}}{y!} \\\\\n&= ((0.7)^{y}e^{-0.7})/(y!), y = 0, 1, 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the probability of each value of $Y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Y = 0) &= ((0.7)^{0}e^{-0.7})/(0!) \\approx 0.496585 \\\\\nP(Y = 1) &= ((0.7)^{1}e^{-0.7})/(1!) \\approx 0.347610 \\\\\nP(Y = 2) &= ((0.7)^{2}e^{-0.7})/(2!) \\approx 0.121663 \\\\\nP(Y \\ge 3) &= 1 - P(Y = 0) - P(Y = 1) - P(Y = 2) = 0.034142\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected frequencies for each observation are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{0} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 0) = 40 \\cdot 0.496585 \\approx 19.8634 \\\\\nE_{1} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 1) = 40 \\cdot 0.347610 \\approx 13.9044 \\\\\nE_{2} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 2) = 40 \\cdot 0.121663 \\approx 4.8665 \\\\\nE_{3} &= N \\cdot P(Y \\ge 3) = 40 \\cdot 0.034142 \\approx 1.3657\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that there are two frequencies less than $5$, so we combine the values $Y = 2$ and $Y=3$.\n\nA table summarizing the observed and corresponding theoretical frequencies is given below:\n\n| $y_i$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\geq2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $O_i$ | $20$ | $12$ | $8$ |\n| $E_i$ | $19.8634$ | $13.9044$ | $6.2322$ |\n| $\\dfrac{(O_i-E_i)^2}{E_i}$ | $0.0009$ | $0.2608$ | $0.5014$ |\n\nWe have rounded values to four decimal places, where appropriate.\n\nWe compute our test statistic by summing the last row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i)((O_{i} - E_{i})^{2})/(E_{i}) &= 0.0009 + 0.2608 + 0.5014 \\\\\n&\\approx 0.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the test statistic is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.8}$ (rounded to one decimal place).\n\nOur data has two constraints: the total number of observations must equal $N$, and the estimated rate $\\hat \\lambda$. Therefore, the number of degrees of freedom $\\nu$ is calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nν &= number of categories - number of constraints \\\\\n&= 3 - 2 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!** We must use the number of categories in the combined table!\n\nSo, there is\n\n$$\n\\nu = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}\n$$\n\ndegree of freedom.\n\nFrom the given chi-square table, the critical value for $\\nu = 1$ at a $10\\%$ significance level is\n\n$$\n\\chi^2_{\\textrm{critical}} = 2.706\n$$\n\nand the critical region is\n\n$$\nX\\geq 2.706\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/15/155070611813bc121d3af67bb5e53c5504bfa3e089a4b0b1d491e8f351f36bc7.png)\n\nOur test statistic $(0.8)$ is not inside the critical region. Therefore, there is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{insufficient}}$ evidence to reject the null hypothesis." 13194139537005 17592186576411 true] [17592186161737 1074 "Suppose we have a set of observed frequencies $O_i$ and corresponding expected frequencies $E_i$, as defined by a theoretical model under the null hypothesis. Then, the random variable\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i} \\frac{(O_i - E_i)^2}{E_i}\n$$\n\napproximately follows a chi-square distribution with $\\nu$ de