92186576411 false] [17592186171790 1074 "We are given a contingency table with $2$ levels for the row factor (Adults and Children) and $2$ for the column factors (Beach and Mountains).\n\nGiven a contingency table with $n$ levels for the row factor $A_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_n$ and $m$ levels for the column factor $B_1, B_2, \\ldots, B_m$, under the hypothesis that the factors are independent, we have that the probability of an individual having the value $A_i$ for the row factor and the value $B_j$ for the column factor is equal to the product of the probabilities:\n\n$$\nP(A_i \\cap B_j) = P(A_i) \\cdot P(B_j), \\qquad i=1,2,\\ldots, n \\quad j=1,2,\\ldots, m\n$$\n\nWe can calculate estimates for the probabilities $P(A_i)$ and $P(B_j)$, which we denote as $\\hat p(A_i)$ and $\\hat p(B_j)$, respectively, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hat{p}(A_{i}) &= \\frac{number of observations with levelA_{i}}{total number of observations} \\\\\n\\hat{p}(B_{j}) &= \\frac{number of observations with levelB_{j}}{total number of observations}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in our case we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hat{p}(Adults) &= \\frac{20 + 28}{80} = \\frac{3}{5} \\\\\n\\hat{p}(Children) &= \\frac{24 + 8}{80} = \\frac{2}{5} \\\\\n\\hat{p}(Beach) &= \\frac{20 + 24}{80} = \\frac{11}{20} \\\\\n\\hat{p}(Mountains) &= \\frac{28 + 8}{80} = \\frac{9}{20}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's estimate the probability of each combination of values under the assumption of independence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Adults∩Beach) &= \\hat{p}(Adults) \\cdot \\hat{p}(Beach) = \\frac{33}{100} \\\\\nP(Adults∩Mountains) &= \\hat{p}(Adults) \\cdot \\hat{p}(Mountains) = \\frac{27}{100} \\\\\nP(Children∩Beach) &= \\hat{p}(Children) \\cdot \\hat{p}(Beach) = \\frac{11}{50} \\\\\nP(Children∩Mountains) &= \\hat{p}(Children) \\cdot \\hat{p}(Mountains) = \\frac{9}{50}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected frequencies for each combination of values under the assumption of independence can be estimated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{11} &= N \\cdot P(Adults∩Beach) = 80 \\cdot \\frac{33}{100} = 26.4 \\\\\nE_{12} &= N \\cdot P(Adults∩Mountains) = 80 \\cdot \\frac{27}{100} = 21.6 \\\\\nE_{21} &= N \\cdot P(Children∩Beach) = 80 \\cdot \\frac{11}{50} = 17.6 \\\\\nE_{22} &= N \\cdot P(Children∩Mountains) = 80 \\cdot \\frac{9}{50} = 14.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA table summarizing the observed and corresponding expected frequencies is given below:\n\n|  | Beach | Mountains |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Adults | $O_{11}=20$<br>$E_{11} =\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}26.4}$ | $O_{12}=28$<br>$E_{12} =\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}21.6}$ |\n| Children | $O_{21}=24$<br>$E_{21} =\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}17.6}$ | $O_{22}=8$<br>$E_{22} =\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}14.4}$ |" 13194139537005 17592186576411 true] [17592186171790 1074 "We are given a contingency table with $2$ levels for the row factor (Adults and Children) and $2$ for the column factors (Beach and Mountains).\n\nGiven a contingency table with $n$ levels for the row factor $A_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_n$ and $m$ levels for the column factor $B_1, B_2, \\ldots, B_m$, under the hypothesis that the factors are independent, we have that the probability of an individual having the value $A_i$ for the row factor and the value $B_j$ for the column factor is equal to the product of the probabilities:\n\n$$\nP(A_i \\cap B_j) = P(A_i) \\cdot P(B_j), \\qquad i=1,2,\\ldots, n \\quad j=1,2,\\ldots, m\n$$\n\nWe can calculate estimates for the probabilities $P(A_i)$ and $P(B_j)$, which we denote as $\\hat p(A_i)$ and $\\hat p(B_j)$, respectively, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^(^)(A_{i}) &= \\frac{number of observations with levelA_{i}}{total number of observations} \\\\\np^(^)(B_{j}) &= \\frac{number of observations with levelB_{j}}{total number of observations}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in our case we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^(^)(Adults) &= \\frac{20 + 28}{80} = \\frac{3}{5} \\\\\np^